I’ll inspect the measurement metadata, pattern, and the provided PXRD tooling first. Then I’ll identify candidate phases, build/refine them in GSAS-II, and validate that every submitted artifact is internally consistent.

The chemistry narrows the credible inventory to Mg–W–O products plus residual MgO/WO₃, but I won’t assume a single-phase MgWO₄ product. I’m now extracting the observed peak set and querying the frozen COD index for all structurally plausible Mg/W/O candidates.

The dominant fingerprint is already clear: every strong line matches monoclinic wolframite MgWO₄ after a common ≈+0.35° 2θ displacement (for example 18.884→19.227°, 23.834→24.215°, and 30.415→30.810°). I’m checking whether the weaker lines all belong to that same phase before adding any residual precursor phase.

All 51 detected peaks—including the weak high-angle lines—index to the same P2/c MgWO₄ cell after the common displacement; I do not see an unassigned MgO or WO₃ fingerprint. I’m proceeding with a one-phase Rietveld model and will only add a second phase if the refined residual develops coherent unmatched reflections.

The residual reveals two additional phases, not just texture mismatch: the 23.43/23.93/24.68° triplet and 33.57/33.88/34.47° group identify monoclinic WO₃, while the isolated 43.21° and 62.54° lines identify MgO. I’m rebuilding the refinement as MgWO₄ + WO₃ + MgO and separating the strongly correlated zero/height terms to keep the calibration physical.

The three-phase model now gives a coherent fit and provisional crystalline fractions near 79% MgWO₄, 14% WO₃, and 7% MgO. The remaining intensity bias is specimen-related (low-angle attenuation is clearly present), so I’m screening the allowed March–Dollase axes before refining coordinates further; unconstrained coordinates otherwise absorb that bias and become chemically implausible.

The phase set is now well supported as MgWO₄ + residual WO₃ + MgO. I’m testing peak-broadening and texture alternatives next, then I’ll lock the smallest defensible model and export the replayable GSAS-II project and requested files.

The broadening screen is deliberately phase-by-phase: it tests whether apparent intensity or fraction shifts are really caused by direction-dependent peak widths. The base three-phase model is already stable; this is the main remaining model-selection check.

The residual’s largest systematic errors sit on the WO₃ triplets near 24° and 33.6°, so the next comparison is structural, not cosmetic: several room-temperature WO₃ database models are being refined under identical conditions. This will decide the WO₃ parent before any coordinate adjustment.

All four WO₃ candidates are still converging through the same staged refinement. I’m keeping atomic coordinates fixed during this comparison so a flexible but incorrect structure cannot win simply by distorting itself.

The WO₃ comparison has separated clearly: COD 2311041 fits substantially better than the coarse 1528915 model at the same parameter count (Rwp 6.30% versus 6.94%). That points to the same monoclinic WO₃ polymorph but a materially better atomic model; the triclinic alternative is still finishing.

The profile now improves markedly when the previously omitted Gaussian U term is refined (Rwp 5.74%, with U ≈ 39). I’m now refining only symmetry-allowed heavy-atom shifts within the generator’s stated perturbation scale; this targets the remaining WO₃ intensity errors without giving the oxygen sublattice enough freedom to overfit.

The texture and uniaxial-strain screens do not show a defensible extra effect: fitted March–Dollase ratios stay near 1 and anisotropic widths do not improve the fit consistently. I’m therefore keeping the parsimonious isotropic model and testing only whether the smooth background needs more flexibility.

The framework confirms the synthetic background is a low-order floor plus either an exponential or power-law tail. I’m fitting those physical forms now, then I’ll lock the defensible background shape and re-refine the three supported phases so the phase fractions aren’t being set by a flexible polynomial.

A replay check exposed an important GSAS-II issue: the saved parameter values had advanced beyond the profile stored in the project, so the displayed 5.74% Rwp was not reproducible from the saved model (fresh calculation: 12.57%). I’m iterating the refinement to parameter/profile consistency before using any fraction or statistic; this is also essential for a valid `final.gpx`.

The stable replay identifies a decaying-tail background decisively; a simple polynomial is ruled out. I’m now comparing the two allowed tail families (bounded power law versus exponential) after full re-refinement, rather than choosing on the background fit alone. Their initial Rwp values differ by only about 0.05 percentage point, so this comparison matters for the minor-phase fractions.

The common displacement parameters are strongly correlated with polarization, and forcing them to their lower bounds drove polarization out of range; that apparent improvement was not replayable. I’m keeping only replay-stable models and testing the remaining shared specimen displacement next, because an incorrect displacement can masquerade as cell strain and push profile/intensity terms toward their bounds.

The replay-stable structure refinement now gives small, physically plausible shifts for Mg/W and the major MgWO₄ oxygen sites, with no coordinate bound hits. The WO₃ tungsten sites are only weakly determined and highly correlated; I’m doing one bounded oxygen-site test to decide whether those coordinates carry real information or should remain explicitly unresolved.

The WO₃ oxygen refinement is substantially slower because it adds 18 correlated coordinates; that behavior itself is a warning sign about identifiability. I’m letting the bounded test finish, but I will reject it if the solution depends on bounds or unstable correlations even if Rwp drops.

The bounded common-U profile likelihood favors MgWO₄/WO₃/MgO values of 0.003/0.012/0.003 Å². The MgO value is weakly constrained, causing about a 1 wt% systematic range in its fraction; I’ll carry that into the report rather than presenting the least-squares precision as absolute. The accepted model is now being given a final consistency pass before export.

The final project passed a read-only GSAS-II replay: all three project phases match the submitted CIFs, the fraction vector is normalized, the full-grid calculated profile reproduces Rwp = 5.8986%, and the replay raised no physical/profile review flags. I’m doing the final artifact/schema checks now.

The deliverables are valid, but the replayed Rwp remains above the Poisson limit (5.90% versus 2.86%). I’m using the remaining residual structure for one last audit of peak-shape anisotropy and phase-specific broadening; I’ll only update the submitted model if a change is replay-stable and physically identifiable.

The phase identities are stable. The residual audit has now found a statistically meaningful, generator-consistent profile correction—weak preferred orientation in MgWO₄/WO₃ plus [001] anisotropic microstrain in MgWO₄—so I’m checking the last competing strain model and then will reconverge and re-export the final project and artifacts.

The WO₃ and MgO anisotropy controls collapse back to isotropic values, so I’m excluding those extra parameters. The retained profile terms lower Rwp from 5.90% to about 5.74% with only three added parameters; I’m now re-optimizing the constrained physical background and checking that the phase fractions are stable.

The independent atomic replay is clean: no profile-review flags, Rwp = 5.72495%, and fractions are 77.73% MgWO₄, 15.25% WO₃, and 7.02% MgO. The displacement-parameter grid returns the same selected values and shows that MgO carries most of the fraction uncertainty; I’m updating the report and then running format/consistency checks on every deliverable.

Analysis complete. Modeled crystalline fractions:

| Phase | Weight fraction |
|---|---:|
| MgWO₄, wolframite (P 2/c) | 77.73% |
| WO₃ (P 2₁/c) | 15.25% |
| MgO, periclase (F m -3 m) | 7.02% |

Fresh GSAS-II replay gives Rwp = 5.7249%, Rp = 4.1400%, and GOF = 2.004. All CIFs passed structural/physical checks and exactly match the project replay exports. MgO has the largest fraction uncertainty, approximately ±1.2 percentage points; amorphous content was not quantified.

